    "body": "There is also an event path truncating behavior we agreed on, so this applies to other situations where the event target is a descendant of the disabled form control.\n\nIf the event target is a descendant of a disabled form control, the disabled form control and all of its ancestors are removed from the event path.\n\nYou can try testing it out in chromium with this demo, you should see that `<div id=child>` gets all the click/mouse events, but the disabled form control and its ancestors don't:\n```html\n<div id=parent>\n  <button disabled>\n    <div id=child>text</div>\n  </button>\n</div>\n\n<script>\nconst button = document.querySelector('button');\nconst parent = document.getElementById('parent');\nconst child = document.getElementById('child');\n[button, parent, child].forEach(element => {\n  ['mousedown', 'mouseup', 'click'].forEach(eventName => {\n    element.addEventListener(eventName, () => {\n      console.log(eventName, element);\n    });\n  });\n});\n</script>\n```",
